# The yamlover language - the self-contained spec. Root body: what yamlover is, the name,
# and the five parts (each a subdirectory chapter, ordered by the pointer array below).
# Sources: YAMLOVER.md, URIs.md, SEPARATOR.md, TYPES.md, META.md, CONCRETES.md, QUERY.md -
# progress in ../../DOCSMIGRATION.md.
The yamlover language
description: the full spec – principles, the abstract model, concretes, pointers, queries
- >
  yamlover is *not* a superset of YAML—it is a distinct but closely related language: similar in surface,
  but with different semantics. Because of these differences, it requires its own parser.
  Reading is **concrete-aware**: a `.yaml`/`.yml` file is parsed with YAML’s semantics, a `.yo` file with
  yamlover’s semantics—each is faithfully read into the same concrete-agnostic representation.

- >
  The abstract model, generated (in computer or human memory) when parsing the language,
  is called the abstract or logical model. This is described in the [Model subchapter](:model).

- >
  A text or other substrate that is read to build the model is called a "concrete" (expression) or, simply, the language.
  Yamlover supports many concretes, including JSON, YAML, and filesystem directories. They are
  all parsed into the same abstract logical model. The various concretes are described in the [Concretes subchapter](:concretes).

- >
  The main concrete used to express the yamlover model is called yamlover itself, or `yamlover/code`.
  Only `yamlover/code` allows to express all features of yamlover graph logical model, other concretes
  have limitations.

- >
  This chapter is the self-contained specification, in five parts: the [abstract
  model](::language:model)—omnivorous nodes with ordered relations and their terminology
  (the type system now lives in the [meta chapter](::meta));
  the [concretes](::language:concretes)—every way a node is physically stored and
  written; [pointers and queries](::language:pointers)—paths, bookmarks, and the query language
  (of which these are the only fragment); the [general
  principles](::language:principles) from which the rest follows; and the [relationship to
  YAML](::language:vs-yaml), covering similarities and differences.
